00:00:29.545 --> 00:00:30.546
That's our goal today.

00:00:30.546 --> 00:00:34.016
It's right there,
discovery of the electron.

00:00:34.016 --> 00:00:36.652
And we're going to start talking
about models of the atom.

00:00:36.652 --> 00:00:39.322
Before we do that,
I want to count.

00:00:39.322 --> 00:00:42.858
I want to count, because
that's what we did on Friday.

00:00:42.858 --> 00:00:48.097
And we'll use this problem
actually twice today.

00:00:48.097 --> 00:00:51.167
Either way, like if I
do a problem in class,

00:00:51.167 --> 00:00:54.170
and it's in a goody bag
and stuff, I don't know,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:56.739
it might be the kind of thing
you might be asked on a quiz.

00:00:56.739 --> 00:00:58.941
I'm just saying.

00:00:58.941 --> 00:01:01.644
So that's good to know.

00:01:01.644 --> 00:01:04.980
If I ask you this question,
because we counted on Friday,

00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:06.248
we learned how to count.

00:01:06.248 --> 00:01:08.718
And we learned about the
mole and Avogadro's number

00:01:08.718 --> 00:01:13.990
and how that gets us into and
out of the atomic world, right?

00:01:13.990 --> 00:01:15.658
So if I say, how
many gallium atoms

00:01:15.658 --> 00:01:18.094
are this strip of gallium?

00:01:18.094 --> 00:01:19.495
Well, we can do that now, right?

00:01:19.495 --> 00:01:23.633
Because well, how do you start?

00:01:23.633 --> 00:01:26.769
This is where you start.

00:01:26.769 --> 00:01:29.138
And you are all taking it
with you everywhere now.

00:01:29.138 --> 00:01:31.073
Remember on Friday,
we talked about how

00:01:31.073 --> 00:01:36.212
you got to bring it with you
everywhere and tweet about it

00:01:36.212 --> 00:01:40.116
and send me pictures
and all that.

00:01:40.116 --> 00:01:42.585
I need the density, right?

00:01:42.585 --> 00:01:43.619
I need the density.

00:01:43.619 --> 00:01:44.854
Density in the periodic table.

00:01:44.854 --> 00:01:46.088
Let's start with the density.

00:01:46.088 --> 00:01:50.292
So if I have the
density of gallium

00:01:50.292 --> 00:01:56.032
and I looked that up as 5.9
grams per centimeter cubed.

00:01:56.032 --> 00:01:57.500
OK, that's good.

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.069
Well, you know, from the
strip I can measure it

00:02:00.069 --> 00:02:02.905
with the super most
accurate ruler in the world

00:02:02.905 --> 00:02:05.741
that you have now in your
hands from the goody bag.

00:02:05.741 --> 00:02:09.145
And you see those
are the measurements.

00:02:09.145 --> 00:02:14.083
And so if you measure
it, you have a volume.

00:02:14.083 --> 00:02:16.719
OK, it's those three
numbers multiplied.

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:17.720
I won't write it out.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:21.590
It's 0.5 centimeters cubed.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.226
And so now with the
density and the volume,

00:02:24.226 --> 00:02:27.530
I get that that
strip has 2.95 grams.

00:02:27.530 --> 00:02:28.798
OK?

00:02:28.798 --> 00:02:30.332
Now that sounds pretty good.

00:02:30.332 --> 00:02:34.837
It's promising, but I want
the number of atoms, right?

00:02:34.837 --> 00:02:36.072
I want the number of atoms.

00:02:36.072 --> 00:02:43.112
And that's where, again, you
go to here, because in there

00:02:43.112 --> 00:02:47.716
is the grams per mole and also
the atomic mass units, which,

00:02:47.716 --> 00:02:50.418
as we talked about Friday,
are the same thing.

00:02:50.418 --> 00:02:51.387
Right?

00:02:51.387 --> 00:02:58.160
And so if I look that up,
I have that for gallium,

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:08.571
we have 69.723 grams
per mole, right?

00:03:08.571 --> 00:03:16.579
And so that means
that in 2.95 grams,

00:03:16.579 --> 00:03:28.023
I have 2.95 divided by
that number, 69.723--

00:03:28.023 --> 00:03:30.059
let's raise this up a little--

00:03:30.059 --> 00:03:37.031
which equals 0.0423 moles.

00:03:37.031 --> 00:03:41.137
I still have not answered the
question because the question

00:03:41.137 --> 00:03:42.838
says, how many atoms?

00:03:42.838 --> 00:03:43.339
Right?

00:03:43.339 --> 00:03:50.212
But that's where n sub a, or
the mole, or this dozen, right?

00:03:50.212 --> 00:03:51.180
But it's not a dozen.

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.281
It's a much bigger number.

00:03:52.281 --> 00:03:55.484
But this number, Avogadro's
number, comes in.

00:03:55.484 --> 00:03:58.387
And so in that
strip of gallium--

00:03:58.387 --> 00:03:59.288
ah!

00:03:59.288 --> 00:04:00.623
No.

00:04:00.623 --> 00:04:07.930
In that strip of gallium,
you've got, in the GA strip,

00:04:07.930 --> 00:04:19.774
you've got 0.0423
times na atoms.

00:04:19.774 --> 00:04:26.549
That's something like, you know,
2.5 times 10 to the 22nd atoms.

00:04:26.549 --> 00:04:29.785
That's a lot of atoms in that
strip, but I was able to do.

00:04:29.785 --> 00:04:32.021
But the secret is the--

00:04:32.021 --> 00:04:33.255
right, remember?

00:04:33.255 --> 00:04:40.262
One atomic mass unit
gets you back and forth

00:04:40.262 --> 00:04:43.532
between one gram per mole.

00:04:43.532 --> 00:04:46.468
And by the way, the atomic
mass unit is nothing.

00:04:46.468 --> 00:04:49.071
It's just a mass unit.

00:04:49.071 --> 00:04:52.608
I mean, one AMU happens
to be, by the way,

00:04:52.608 --> 00:05:02.351
oh, roughly 1.66 times 10
to the minus 27th kilograms.

00:05:02.351 --> 00:05:03.385
Huh!

00:05:03.385 --> 00:05:06.822
That works because now you can
multiply this by a whole mole,

00:05:06.822 --> 00:05:09.325
and it's a gram.

00:05:09.325 --> 00:05:11.660
Back and forth, OK?

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:13.329
Good counting.

00:05:13.329 --> 00:05:14.430
Starting with counting.

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:16.098
We're going to
end with counting.

00:05:16.098 --> 00:05:19.668
But in between, we're going to
discover something beautiful,

00:05:19.668 --> 00:05:22.371
because last week--

00:05:22.371 --> 00:05:24.206
OK, the thesis of
this whole class,

00:05:24.206 --> 00:05:26.075
remember, is
electronic structure

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:27.476
holds the key to everything.

00:05:27.476 --> 00:05:30.613
Everything in your
life can be understood

00:05:30.613 --> 00:05:35.117
if you understood the electronic
structure of the elements.

00:05:35.117 --> 00:05:38.120
Week one, last week,
we talked about this.

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:39.855
We built it on Friday.

00:05:39.855 --> 00:05:41.223
We talked about elements.

00:05:41.223 --> 00:05:42.424
We did a few reactions.

00:05:42.424 --> 00:05:44.058
We talked about balancing.

00:05:44.058 --> 00:05:45.728
We made sure we
have enough nitrogen

00:05:45.728 --> 00:05:49.198
to grow plants for 100 million
more years, eliminate reagents.

00:05:49.198 --> 00:05:52.668
This week, we're going
to answer the question,

00:05:52.668 --> 00:05:55.905
why are these things different?

00:05:55.905 --> 00:05:58.040
We're switching gears, right?

00:05:58.040 --> 00:06:00.576
We know that there are
these indivisible atoms now.

00:06:00.576 --> 00:06:02.811
And we built a whole
table of them, right?

00:06:02.811 --> 00:06:03.445
Mendeleev.

00:06:03.445 --> 00:06:05.080
But now we want to
know why they're

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:06.415
different from one another.

00:06:06.415 --> 00:06:07.783
What causes that difference?

00:06:07.783 --> 00:06:12.554
And that starts with the
discovery of the electron.

00:06:12.554 --> 00:06:14.957
And really this is such--

00:06:14.957 --> 00:06:17.693
so let's write this here.

00:06:17.693 --> 00:06:20.296
We'll have this board.

00:06:20.296 --> 00:06:23.465
This is such a great story.

00:06:23.465 --> 00:06:30.306
This is a detective story that
starts with an atom looking--

00:06:30.306 --> 00:06:38.614
so these are atom models, OK?

00:06:38.614 --> 00:06:42.051
So back in, oh, let's say, 18--

00:06:42.051 --> 00:06:43.686
ah, OK, 460.

00:06:43.686 --> 00:06:46.855
If it's Democritus,
it's 460 B.C.

00:06:46.855 --> 00:06:50.826
And then remember, we sort of
went all the way to Dalton,

00:06:50.826 --> 00:06:57.499
who, in 1803, he
said, yup, Democritus,

00:06:57.499 --> 00:06:59.268
we're going with that.

00:06:59.268 --> 00:07:00.369
We're going with that.

00:07:00.369 --> 00:07:02.705
And oh, there are
these indivisible

00:07:02.705 --> 00:07:05.007
things called atoms.

00:07:05.007 --> 00:07:07.009
So that was 1803.

00:07:07.009 --> 00:07:11.780
But we need to understand
them in order to explain this.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:12.881
We need to understand them.

00:07:12.881 --> 00:07:15.084
And so this is a
detective story.

00:07:15.084 --> 00:07:19.288
It's one of the greatest
ones ever, ever, ever

00:07:19.288 --> 00:07:21.123
played out in science.

00:07:21.123 --> 00:07:25.494
It led to nothing less than the
understanding of all of nature.

00:07:25.494 --> 00:07:27.463
OK, now, how did it start?

00:07:27.463 --> 00:07:31.600
Well, it started
with J.J. Thomson.

00:07:31.600 --> 00:07:35.104
Now, so J.J. Thomson--

00:07:35.104 --> 00:07:38.707
by the way, he won the Nobel
Prize for this discovery.

00:07:38.707 --> 00:07:41.043
He's credited with
discovering the electron.

00:07:41.043 --> 00:07:45.214
Also seven of his students
won the Nobel Prize.

00:07:45.214 --> 00:07:46.148
That's cool!

00:07:46.148 --> 00:07:49.618
And I remind my graduate
students of that

00:07:49.618 --> 00:07:54.390
all the time, without trying
to put any pressure on them.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:55.591
How did he do it?

00:07:55.591 --> 00:07:58.594
Well, you see, he
had these things.

00:07:58.594 --> 00:07:59.628
There is his lab.

00:07:59.628 --> 00:08:01.630
There's Thomson,
there's his lab.

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:03.532
And these things were around.

00:08:03.532 --> 00:08:05.567
They're called
cathode ray tubes.

00:08:05.567 --> 00:08:06.535
Cathode ray tubes.

00:08:06.535 --> 00:08:07.870
What do they do?

00:08:07.870 --> 00:08:08.370
All right?

00:08:08.370 --> 00:08:09.038
What do they do?

00:08:09.038 --> 00:08:11.940
Well, your grandparents would
know something about them.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:13.776
I'll talk about that
in a few minutes.

00:08:13.776 --> 00:08:20.282
But Thomson, Thomson
was interested in seeing

00:08:20.282 --> 00:08:21.683
what he saw on this thing here.

00:08:21.683 --> 00:08:23.285
And that's the
photographic plate.

00:08:23.285 --> 00:08:24.887
So what we're going
to do is we're just

00:08:24.887 --> 00:08:26.755
going to draw the
cathode ray tube,

00:08:26.755 --> 00:08:30.626
draw it as best I can here.

00:08:30.626 --> 00:08:36.365
So there is a cathode ray
tube, and over here we've

00:08:36.365 --> 00:08:43.539
got a cathode, and over
here we've got an anode.

00:08:43.539 --> 00:08:50.813
So this would be a cathode,
and this here is an anode.

00:08:50.813 --> 00:08:52.281
OK, good.

00:08:52.281 --> 00:08:55.517
Now, that means that if I hook
the other two pieces of metal,

00:08:55.517 --> 00:08:57.719
two pieces of metal,
and if I hook them up

00:08:57.719 --> 00:09:04.460
to a voltage supply, then
I can get charge on them.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.562
We know that already.

00:09:06.562 --> 00:09:10.265
But what they didn't understand
is what happened next.

00:09:10.265 --> 00:09:13.535
You see, because if you crank
the voltage up really high

00:09:13.535 --> 00:09:16.672
and you put a
phosphor screen here--

00:09:16.672 --> 00:09:18.240
so this was a phosphor screen--

00:09:22.344 --> 00:09:24.813
and if you crank the
voltage up high enough,

00:09:24.813 --> 00:09:28.717
and this is what Thomson did,
you pump the gas out of it.

00:09:28.717 --> 00:09:29.351
Right?

00:09:29.351 --> 00:09:32.387
So you get it so there's
not too much stuff in there.

00:09:32.387 --> 00:09:36.725
Then what happens is
something shot across

00:09:36.725 --> 00:09:38.627
and lit up the screen.

00:09:38.627 --> 00:09:40.629
Something.

00:09:40.629 --> 00:09:42.364
What?

00:09:42.364 --> 00:09:44.967
They really didn't know,
and when you don't know,

00:09:44.967 --> 00:09:47.903
you experiment and you
apply the scientific method.

00:09:47.903 --> 00:09:50.038
And one of the things
that Thomson did

00:09:50.038 --> 00:09:54.409
is he said, well, what if I
put some charge plates here,

00:09:54.409 --> 00:09:56.945
like this and this?

00:09:56.945 --> 00:09:58.614
So I have a [? set, ?] right?

00:09:58.614 --> 00:10:01.083
So it is said, this is going
to have charge that flies off.

00:10:01.083 --> 00:10:03.719
We know that, because we
kind of know what happens.

00:10:03.719 --> 00:10:05.354
But he didn't know
what was happening.

00:10:05.354 --> 00:10:07.756
He's just cranking the voltage
up, and all of a sudden the

00:10:07.756 --> 00:10:08.891
screen lights up.

00:10:08.891 --> 00:10:12.494
So he's like, well, let me put
some charge plates here and see

00:10:12.494 --> 00:10:13.695
what happens.

00:10:13.695 --> 00:10:18.934
And what happened there is
it went like this, right?

00:10:18.934 --> 00:10:22.571
And it struck the
phosphor screen there,

00:10:22.571 --> 00:10:24.339
and it glowed there.

00:10:24.339 --> 00:10:30.913
So that's with charge,
with charge plates.

00:10:30.913 --> 00:10:32.314
Right?

00:10:32.314 --> 00:10:37.386
No charge plates, just
to be perfectly clear.

00:10:37.386 --> 00:10:39.388
And he could play
with this, right?

00:10:39.388 --> 00:10:42.057
And people do play with
this, to my great surprise,

00:10:42.057 --> 00:10:47.196
if you go onto the internet
and you Google this.

00:10:47.196 --> 00:10:50.465
You find people make
these and play with them,

00:10:50.465 --> 00:10:51.433
and that's a magnet.

00:10:51.433 --> 00:10:54.002
This is what it
looks like, right?

00:10:54.002 --> 00:10:58.240
But we didn't know
what this was, right?

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:00.642
So that's a magnet,
and this person

00:11:00.642 --> 00:11:03.845
is just turning over whether
which part of the magnet, where

00:11:03.845 --> 00:11:05.147
the magnetic field is oriented.

00:11:05.147 --> 00:11:08.217
And you can see, you can
really mess with this.

00:11:08.217 --> 00:11:10.819
What could it be?

00:11:10.819 --> 00:11:13.422
It's responding to
an external field.

00:11:13.422 --> 00:11:21.396
It's responding to an external
field, so we know right away,

00:11:21.396 --> 00:11:32.674
this tells us that atoms are
not the ultimate form of matter.

00:11:39.448 --> 00:11:42.751
Atoms were not the
ultimate form of matter.

00:11:42.751 --> 00:11:48.624
That was a big deal, because
the atoms here were neutral.

00:11:48.624 --> 00:11:52.094
They didn't respond, so this
must be something else that's

00:11:52.094 --> 00:11:55.430
flying off and responding,
that I can see visually

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:57.332
on this photographic plate.

00:11:57.332 --> 00:11:59.268
It must be something else.

00:11:59.268 --> 00:12:01.336
Well, he was also--

00:12:01.336 --> 00:12:05.874
Thomson was-- oh, ha!

00:12:05.874 --> 00:12:09.211
Going down here, I'm
going to use this

00:12:09.211 --> 00:12:12.781
so it's right next to it.

00:12:12.781 --> 00:12:17.552
He was not able to measure
the mass of these things,

00:12:17.552 --> 00:12:19.888
but he was able,
using a combination

00:12:19.888 --> 00:12:24.760
of magnetic and electric fields
and the little bit of Maxwell,

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:29.665
some ENM, he was able to deduce
the charge to mass ratio.

00:12:29.665 --> 00:12:30.832
So this falls over here.

00:12:30.832 --> 00:12:35.304
I'll put it here, from Thomson.

00:12:35.304 --> 00:12:41.943
So he was able to get
the charge to mass ratio.

00:12:44.513 --> 00:12:52.187
And he was able to measure
that at minus 1.76 times 10

00:12:52.187 --> 00:12:56.290
to the 11th coulombs
per kilogram.

00:12:56.290 --> 00:13:00.562
Now, he knew it was negatively
charged because of this, right?

00:13:00.562 --> 00:13:02.464
He put the plates
on, and it wanted

00:13:02.464 --> 00:13:04.900
to avoid the negative plate.

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.703
So they knew their
ENM at the time.

00:13:07.703 --> 00:13:10.772
They knew that the negative
charges would repel.

00:13:10.772 --> 00:13:12.708
So he knew that it
was a negative charge.

00:13:12.708 --> 00:13:17.579
Now, the last thing, oh, is this
one important for chemistry!

00:13:17.579 --> 00:13:20.515
He would swap in different
materials, different metals

00:13:20.515 --> 00:13:21.750
for the cathode and anode.

00:13:21.750 --> 00:13:23.118
For the cathode especially.

00:13:23.118 --> 00:13:25.921
And the results were the same.

00:13:25.921 --> 00:13:27.723
The results were the same.

00:13:27.723 --> 00:13:29.257
Oh!

00:13:29.257 --> 00:13:42.671
Independent,
independent of metal.

00:13:42.671 --> 00:13:44.106
Independent of the metal.

00:13:44.106 --> 00:13:48.377
That meant this
thing that he was

00:13:48.377 --> 00:13:50.579
observing for the first
time was fundamental.

00:13:50.579 --> 00:13:53.415
There's something
fundamental about it.

00:13:53.415 --> 00:13:54.549
Fundamental!

00:14:03.759 --> 00:14:05.360
This was a really big deal.

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.329
This was a really big deal.

00:14:07.329 --> 00:14:12.567
And it really opened up
the idea that the atom

00:14:12.567 --> 00:14:14.936
had something else in it.

00:14:14.936 --> 00:14:26.448
Now, see, Thomson knew that the
atom was neutral, like I said.

00:14:26.448 --> 00:14:27.849
So you've got these
charged things

00:14:27.849 --> 00:14:31.019
coming off of these
atoms, which are neutral.

00:14:31.019 --> 00:14:33.655
That means that it must consist
of these charged particles.

00:14:33.655 --> 00:14:35.891
But if it's neutral, it's
got to have the other charge

00:14:35.891 --> 00:14:38.427
of particle in it as well.

00:14:38.427 --> 00:14:41.196
And so the detective
story goes on.

00:14:41.196 --> 00:14:45.700
So this would be
Thomson around 18--

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:47.836
oh, things really picked up.

00:14:47.836 --> 00:14:49.738
1807.

00:14:49.738 --> 00:14:53.775
And he said, well,
OK, these electrons

00:14:53.775 --> 00:14:56.077
must be inside of the atom.

00:14:56.077 --> 00:14:58.246
That's how I'm liberating them.

00:14:58.246 --> 00:15:00.649
Crank up the voltage, and
they must be in there.

00:15:00.649 --> 00:15:03.018
But so must positive charges.

00:15:03.018 --> 00:15:05.620
So must positive charges, OK?

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:07.022
And that was Thomson's model.

00:15:07.022 --> 00:15:10.926
So at that time,
you could really

00:15:10.926 --> 00:15:13.128
have this picture of
stuff inside the atom.

00:15:13.128 --> 00:15:17.732
Now, like I said, he
couldn't measure--

00:15:17.732 --> 00:15:20.936
they didn't have a
scale good enough

00:15:20.936 --> 00:15:22.437
to measure the mass
of the electron.

00:15:22.437 --> 00:15:25.006
He could get it out, all right?

00:15:25.006 --> 00:15:31.012
But it was Millikan and
his very famous experiments

00:15:31.012 --> 00:15:35.317
called the oil drop experiments
that gave us the actual charge.

00:15:35.317 --> 00:15:37.919
And then from this
ratio, we can get

00:15:37.919 --> 00:15:41.356
the mass of this
mystery thing that

00:15:41.356 --> 00:15:43.258
was coming out of atoms, OK?

00:15:43.258 --> 00:15:49.931
And instead of trying to
draw the Millikan experiment,

00:15:49.931 --> 00:15:53.602
I found a wonderful, short,
like one-ish minute video,

00:15:53.602 --> 00:15:55.370
which I'll play,
because it shows how

00:15:55.370 --> 00:15:57.172
the Millikan experiment worked.

00:15:57.172 --> 00:15:58.206
It's really cool.

00:15:58.206 --> 00:15:58.707
Oh!

00:15:58.707 --> 00:15:59.207
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:15:59.207 --> 00:16:01.676
- Robert Millikan, working
at the University of Chicago,

00:16:01.676 --> 00:16:04.179
succeeded in measuring the
charge on the electron.

00:16:04.179 --> 00:16:05.046
That's not happening.

00:16:05.046 --> 00:16:08.383
- He allowed the fine spray of
oil to settle through a hole,

00:16:08.383 --> 00:16:11.152
into a chamber where he
could observe their fall.

00:16:11.152 --> 00:16:13.088
The top and bottom
of the chamber

00:16:13.088 --> 00:16:15.524
consisted of electrically
charged plates.

00:16:15.524 --> 00:16:18.193
He introduced a
source of x-rays which

00:16:18.193 --> 00:16:21.363
can cause creation of charges
when they strike matter.

00:16:21.363 --> 00:16:25.233
Charges reduced by the x-rays
attached to an oil droplet,

00:16:25.233 --> 00:16:29.170
and producing one or more
charges on the droplet.

00:16:29.170 --> 00:16:32.741
When there is no voltage of
light, the fall of the droplets

00:16:32.741 --> 00:16:34.943
is determined by the mass
and the viscosity of air

00:16:34.943 --> 00:16:36.845
through which they fall.

00:16:36.845 --> 00:16:39.214
When a voltage is
applied, the droplets

00:16:39.214 --> 00:16:42.183
that have a negative charge
will fall more slowly,

00:16:42.183 --> 00:16:45.620
stop falling, or even rise,
depending on the number

00:16:45.620 --> 00:16:47.188
of charges on them.

00:16:47.188 --> 00:16:50.525
By adjusting the applied voltage
and observing the droplets,

00:16:50.525 --> 00:16:52.994
both with voltage
off and voltage

00:16:52.994 --> 00:16:55.497
on, Millikan was
able to determine

00:16:55.497 --> 00:16:57.566
that the charges on
the droplets were

00:16:57.566 --> 00:17:01.636
all multiples on a
smallest value, 1.6 times

00:17:01.636 --> 00:17:04.940
10 to the minus 19th Coulombs.

00:17:04.940 --> 00:17:08.009
He took this to be the
charge on a single electron.

00:17:08.009 --> 00:17:08.810
[END PLAYBACK]

00:17:08.810 --> 00:17:11.112
All right, so now you see
that that would have been--

00:17:11.112 --> 00:17:12.347
I hope that was--

00:17:12.347 --> 00:17:14.082
could you hear that in the back?

00:17:14.082 --> 00:17:15.016
Sort of, OK.

00:17:15.016 --> 00:17:15.517
All right.

00:17:15.517 --> 00:17:17.319
Oh, I got this.

00:17:19.788 --> 00:17:21.222
You know, that
would have been hard

00:17:21.222 --> 00:17:22.991
for me to draw the
animation, but what

00:17:22.991 --> 00:17:24.224
a brilliant experiment, right?

00:17:24.224 --> 00:17:27.762
So spray some oil
in a container, OK?

00:17:27.762 --> 00:17:28.930
Try to get the drop small.

00:17:28.930 --> 00:17:30.799
You don't know how small
they're going to be.

00:17:30.799 --> 00:17:31.800
And then as they fall--

00:17:31.800 --> 00:17:34.502
OK, have an electric
field inside there.

00:17:34.502 --> 00:17:38.907
And then as they fall, zip
them, zap them to charge them.

00:17:38.907 --> 00:17:40.942
You also don't know
how much charge

00:17:40.942 --> 00:17:43.044
you're putting on them, right?

00:17:43.044 --> 00:17:44.279
But you're charging them.

00:17:44.279 --> 00:17:45.880
And if they're charged
and they're in a field,

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:48.416
and there are these tiny, little
microscopic droplets of oil,

00:17:48.416 --> 00:17:50.418
they're going to maybe slow
down or suspend or maybe even go

00:17:50.418 --> 00:17:50.952
the other way.

00:17:50.952 --> 00:17:53.254
And what he observed by doing
this over and over again,

00:17:53.254 --> 00:17:57.592
that there was some multiple
that you never got below,

00:17:57.592 --> 00:17:58.960
right?

00:17:58.960 --> 00:18:01.963
And you couldn't say
necessarily for 100% certainty

00:18:01.963 --> 00:18:03.965
that it was the fundamental
charge of electrons.

00:18:03.965 --> 00:18:05.300
It might have been
a multiple of that.

00:18:05.300 --> 00:18:06.468
But it was pretty clear.

00:18:06.468 --> 00:18:07.836
You could never get below that.

00:18:07.836 --> 00:18:11.673
So that was the charge discovery
in this detective story,

00:18:11.673 --> 00:18:17.212
and it allowed us to understand
that the electron had a mass

00:18:17.212 --> 00:18:18.713
and it had a charge.

00:18:18.713 --> 00:18:20.448
And that we knew both
of them at the time.

00:18:20.448 --> 00:18:25.787
So at the time then, we
already had a much deeper

00:18:25.787 --> 00:18:27.555
understanding, if
you think about it,

00:18:27.555 --> 00:18:31.292
just four years later than
the time of Dalton because

00:18:31.292 --> 00:18:32.527
of these two experiments.

00:18:32.527 --> 00:18:33.194
Yeah?

00:18:33.194 --> 00:18:39.467
So we said that Thomson was
56-- he'd be [INAUDIBLE] 1940s,

00:18:39.467 --> 00:18:41.436
so wouldn't that be 1907?

00:18:41.436 --> 00:18:43.471
1856 to huh?

00:18:43.471 --> 00:18:45.240
Thomson was alive.

00:18:45.240 --> 00:18:45.974
Alive.

00:18:45.974 --> 00:18:48.376
[INAUDIBLE]

00:18:48.376 --> 00:18:50.779
I think I read in my--

00:18:50.779 --> 00:18:51.846
he was ahead of his time.

00:18:51.846 --> 00:18:52.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:52.380 --> 00:18:54.115
Thank you very much.

00:18:54.115 --> 00:18:56.718
I think I read this
wrong, and that's

00:18:56.718 --> 00:18:58.820
because I don't
have my glasses on.

00:19:02.323 --> 00:19:04.192
So now that I look
at that, it wasn't

00:19:04.192 --> 00:19:06.027
that small of a distance.

00:19:06.027 --> 00:19:09.330
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:09.330 --> 00:19:12.400
Thank you very much.

00:19:12.400 --> 00:19:13.268
You make mistakes.

00:19:13.268 --> 00:19:17.305
You make mistakes, and
that is how you learn!

00:19:17.305 --> 00:19:20.608
[LAUGHTER]

00:19:20.608 --> 00:19:24.312
You don't make progress.

00:19:24.312 --> 00:19:27.115
Progress has almost
nothing to do with success.

00:19:27.115 --> 00:19:27.816
I mean this.

00:19:27.816 --> 00:19:29.651
I'm using my own mistake
here as an example.

00:19:29.651 --> 00:19:31.586
Progress has almost
nothing to do with success.

00:19:31.586 --> 00:19:33.722
Progress has only
to do with what

00:19:33.722 --> 00:19:34.956
you choose to do with failure.

00:19:37.826 --> 00:19:39.327
Did somebody just say, whoa?

00:19:39.327 --> 00:19:40.195
Thank you.

00:19:40.195 --> 00:19:40.862
Thank you.

00:19:40.862 --> 00:19:42.363
That hit me here.

00:19:42.363 --> 00:19:44.532
All right.

00:19:44.532 --> 00:19:46.000
OK, let's do a why this matters.

00:19:46.000 --> 00:19:49.170
Let's do a why this matters.

00:19:49.170 --> 00:19:50.038
Why does this matter?

00:19:50.038 --> 00:19:53.608
Well, because, see,
these guys were

00:19:53.608 --> 00:19:56.678
trying to figure out what
was inside of an atom.

00:19:56.678 --> 00:19:59.380
But other people,
like John Baird,

00:19:59.380 --> 00:20:00.448
said, well, wait a second.

00:20:00.448 --> 00:20:02.617
You just gave me a paintbrush.

00:20:02.617 --> 00:20:04.185
You gave me a paintbrush.

00:20:04.185 --> 00:20:05.120
Look at this.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:09.190
That's a paintbrush,
painting with a magnet.

00:20:09.190 --> 00:20:09.891
It really is.

00:20:09.891 --> 00:20:12.127
And the screen would
light up over here.

00:20:12.127 --> 00:20:14.929
And so he said,
well, I can paint.

00:20:14.929 --> 00:20:16.731
I can paint pictures.

00:20:16.731 --> 00:20:19.467
And this really is the
first television screen.

00:20:19.467 --> 00:20:22.537
This was the first TV
screen, and all they

00:20:22.537 --> 00:20:23.705
needed to do, there--

00:20:23.705 --> 00:20:26.674
OK, right?

00:20:26.674 --> 00:20:27.442
There it is.

00:20:27.442 --> 00:20:27.942
Look!

00:20:27.942 --> 00:20:30.612
That's a cathode ray tube.

00:20:30.612 --> 00:20:35.817
Maybe that's one of Thomson's
students who won a Nobel Prize.

00:20:35.817 --> 00:20:38.386
And but now, you put
this down, and you put

00:20:38.386 --> 00:20:39.687
these sort of things around it.

00:20:39.687 --> 00:20:40.789
What are those things?

00:20:40.789 --> 00:20:43.091
Magnetic fields,
that's all it is.

00:20:43.091 --> 00:20:44.225
It's just magnetic fields.

00:20:44.225 --> 00:20:46.461
It's Thomson's
experiments, right?

00:20:46.461 --> 00:20:51.933
But now they're using it
to zip the beam around

00:20:51.933 --> 00:20:54.636
faster than you're
[INAUDIBLE] keep up with so

00:20:54.636 --> 00:20:55.870
that it looks like a picture.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:59.474
Now, electron painting had
never been done before,

00:20:59.474 --> 00:21:03.411
because we didn't know that
we had these electrons.

00:21:03.411 --> 00:21:05.680
But as soon as we
knew, boy, did that

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:10.585
launch a completely
new era of screens.

00:21:10.585 --> 00:21:11.085
Right?

00:21:11.085 --> 00:21:12.787
The era of screens.

00:21:12.787 --> 00:21:14.722
We don't use cathode ray tubes.

00:21:14.722 --> 00:21:16.491
I was going to say,
ask your grandparents.

00:21:16.491 --> 00:21:18.293
They'll tell you about
the cathode ray tube

00:21:18.293 --> 00:21:22.230
TVs, which they all had.

00:21:22.230 --> 00:21:25.700
But you know, we don't
use cathode ray tubes

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:28.703
to paint with electrons
today in that way,

00:21:28.703 --> 00:21:31.739
but we still paint
with electrons today.

00:21:31.739 --> 00:21:32.407
Right?

00:21:32.407 --> 00:21:35.743
Youre OLED screen
is still simply

00:21:35.743 --> 00:21:40.381
an electron-based
painting tool, right?

00:21:40.381 --> 00:21:42.016
OK, we're just
pumping the electrons

00:21:42.016 --> 00:21:45.086
into the phosphor
in a different way,

00:21:45.086 --> 00:21:47.288
and we'll be talking
about that as we

00:21:47.288 --> 00:21:49.357
go through the rest of
this week and we understand

00:21:49.357 --> 00:21:52.060
how electrons
interact with light

00:21:52.060 --> 00:21:54.762
coming in and out of an atom.

00:21:54.762 --> 00:21:57.532
OK, so that's my
why this matters.

00:21:57.532 --> 00:21:59.234
And by the way, a
side note here is

00:21:59.234 --> 00:22:05.106
that when TVs first came
along, green was pretty easy.

00:22:05.106 --> 00:22:08.776
There were a lot of chemistries
that were used for this screen.

00:22:08.776 --> 00:22:10.378
You put a different
chemistry here,

00:22:10.378 --> 00:22:13.848
and it lights up differently
when electrons hit it.

00:22:13.848 --> 00:22:14.482
Why?

00:22:14.482 --> 00:22:17.418
Wait until Wednesday.

00:22:17.418 --> 00:22:18.553
Green was easy.

00:22:18.553 --> 00:22:19.254
Yellow was easy.

00:22:19.254 --> 00:22:20.421
Red was hard.

00:22:20.421 --> 00:22:21.723
Red was hard.

00:22:21.723 --> 00:22:23.424
They couldn't get a good red.

00:22:23.424 --> 00:22:28.529
And of course, that's
essentially the reason

00:22:28.529 --> 00:22:32.934
there were no color TVs
until the '60s, right?

00:22:32.934 --> 00:22:35.303
And the answer, of
course, was here.

00:22:35.303 --> 00:22:38.806
The answer was that there
was a phosphor that worked,

00:22:38.806 --> 00:22:43.111
but it was yttrium orthovanadate
with a little bit of europium

00:22:43.111 --> 00:22:44.946
added to it.

00:22:44.946 --> 00:22:46.180
Just a little bit of europium.

00:22:46.180 --> 00:22:47.048
Why did that work?

00:22:47.048 --> 00:22:49.884
Again, we need to understand how
electrons interact with matter,

00:22:49.884 --> 00:22:52.120
which is where we're going.

00:22:52.120 --> 00:22:56.224
And speaking of yttrium,
speaking of yttrium,

00:22:56.224 --> 00:22:59.127
this is also a side story,
but it's kind of worth noting.

00:22:59.127 --> 00:23:01.963
You know, elements are
named often-- well,

00:23:01.963 --> 00:23:04.565
elements can be named
after many things, right?

00:23:04.565 --> 00:23:08.202
In this case, it was named
after Ytterby, Sweden.

00:23:08.202 --> 00:23:10.471
Ytterby, Sweden, is
a pretty cool place.

00:23:10.471 --> 00:23:12.473
They had this one cave.

00:23:12.473 --> 00:23:13.708
You got to go to it.

00:23:13.708 --> 00:23:17.245
It's really cool, because four
elements of the periodic table

00:23:17.245 --> 00:23:19.714
were all discovered
from Ytterby, Sweden,

00:23:19.714 --> 00:23:21.883
in this one cave.

00:23:21.883 --> 00:23:22.717
Four!

00:23:22.717 --> 00:23:24.919
And I keep thinking, where
is that cave in Cambridge?

00:23:24.919 --> 00:23:26.454
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:26.454 --> 00:23:28.056
Hm?

00:23:28.056 --> 00:23:29.924
They cannot discover
any more elements,

00:23:29.924 --> 00:23:33.261
because there's no other way
to mess with the name Ytterby.

00:23:33.261 --> 00:23:35.196
[LAUGHTER]

00:23:35.196 --> 00:23:38.199
That's pretty cool.

00:23:38.199 --> 00:23:38.967
That's pretty cool.

00:23:38.967 --> 00:23:40.868
That's a cool cave.

00:23:40.868 --> 00:23:42.203
That's worth visiting.

00:23:42.203 --> 00:23:45.640
All right, so the structure
of the atom in the 1900s--

00:23:45.640 --> 00:23:47.075
thank you for the
date correction.

00:23:47.075 --> 00:23:51.312
1900, the structure of the
atom was as follows, all right?

00:23:51.312 --> 00:23:54.716
The atom is
electrically neutral,

00:23:54.716 --> 00:23:56.784
but there are these
negative charges.

00:23:56.784 --> 00:23:59.020
These negative charges
called electrons.

00:23:59.020 --> 00:24:00.888
The electron has
a very small mass.

00:24:00.888 --> 00:24:04.759
We know that from the oil
drop experiments, right?

00:24:04.759 --> 00:24:06.527
Which means that
the bulk of the atom

00:24:06.527 --> 00:24:10.398
is positive, because the bulk
of the mass of the atom--

00:24:10.398 --> 00:24:12.500
you know, the atom
weighs one thing.

00:24:12.500 --> 00:24:14.102
The electron is
just a small part

00:24:14.102 --> 00:24:19.374
of that in terms of the mass,
so most of the mass is positive.

00:24:19.374 --> 00:24:21.509
And there was a question.

00:24:21.509 --> 00:24:22.010
OK?

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:24.178
So we're getting
somewhere, but how

00:24:24.178 --> 00:24:26.681
do these charges really look?

00:24:26.681 --> 00:24:29.550
How do these charges
arrange inside of this atom?

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:30.785
Is Thomson really right?

00:24:30.785 --> 00:24:33.821
And that was the next part
of our detective story,

00:24:33.821 --> 00:24:35.823
was to really understand
what these charges are

00:24:35.823 --> 00:24:36.824
doing inside the atom.

00:24:36.824 --> 00:24:38.526
I'm carrying this
around like my security

00:24:38.526 --> 00:24:42.363
blanket, which is making me
feel very secure right now.

00:24:42.363 --> 00:24:44.632
And you should all carry it too.

00:24:44.632 --> 00:24:49.771
This part of the story relies
on another type of energy

00:24:49.771 --> 00:24:53.975
that was being
discovered at the time.

00:24:53.975 --> 00:24:58.479
And you all may recognize
this as radiation.

00:24:58.479 --> 00:25:05.720
Now, radioactivity,
radioactivity

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:10.591
is nothing more
than ray activity.

00:25:10.591 --> 00:25:14.996
This is what they named
it after, ray activity.

00:25:17.632 --> 00:25:19.700
That's radioactivity.

00:25:19.700 --> 00:25:24.038
And it was these three
people who really pioneered

00:25:24.038 --> 00:25:26.074
the understanding-- gesundheit--

00:25:26.074 --> 00:25:29.077
of radioactivity
and in particular,

00:25:29.077 --> 00:25:32.580
the fact that some elements
seemed to just be radioactive.

00:25:32.580 --> 00:25:33.381
What did that mean?

00:25:33.381 --> 00:25:37.819
Well, it means they gave
off these rays of energy.

00:25:37.819 --> 00:25:39.921
That means they gave off
these rays of energy.

00:25:39.921 --> 00:25:42.323
You had Henri Becquerel.

00:25:42.323 --> 00:25:45.960
You had Marie Curie
and Pierre Curie.

00:25:45.960 --> 00:25:47.195
Notice I didn't even try.

00:25:47.195 --> 00:25:48.029
Where's Jerome?

00:25:48.029 --> 00:25:50.932
I didn't even try
with the French.

00:25:50.932 --> 00:25:57.205
But anyway, they took these
materials, like uranium ore,

00:25:57.205 --> 00:25:59.507
minerals made out of
uranium, and they said,

00:25:59.507 --> 00:26:02.610
this stuff looks like it's got
something coming off of it.

00:26:02.610 --> 00:26:05.947
Let's see if we can find what
elements are causing that.

00:26:05.947 --> 00:26:07.482
All right?

00:26:07.482 --> 00:26:13.588
And Marie Curie, she found
polonium and radium this way,

00:26:13.588 --> 00:26:14.388
those two elements.

00:26:14.388 --> 00:26:18.092
Polonium named after her home
country, her native country

00:26:18.092 --> 00:26:20.695
of Poland.

00:26:20.695 --> 00:26:24.899
And this stuff gave
off all this energy,

00:26:24.899 --> 00:26:27.735
and it was so energetic they
could literally put it in a cup

00:26:27.735 --> 00:26:30.805
and have some writing there and
put it in a super-dark room,

00:26:30.805 --> 00:26:33.141
and it would
illuminate the picture.

00:26:33.141 --> 00:26:33.674
Right?

00:26:33.674 --> 00:26:35.009
It would illuminate the picture.

00:26:35.009 --> 00:26:40.281
But this is not a recommended
way to light your pictures.

00:26:40.281 --> 00:26:48.389
So Pierre carried a vial of
radium, because it was so cool

00:26:48.389 --> 00:26:50.458
and it glowed!

00:26:50.458 --> 00:26:52.693
He was like, look at this stuff!

00:26:52.693 --> 00:26:53.394
Right?

00:26:53.394 --> 00:26:57.465
And he actually did have
bouts of radiation poisoning,

00:26:57.465 --> 00:26:59.934
although he died,
tragically, when

00:26:59.934 --> 00:27:04.105
he was struck by a
horse-drawn carriage in 1906.

00:27:04.105 --> 00:27:07.408
Marie lived much
longer and won two--

00:27:07.408 --> 00:27:09.744
but also died because
of radiation poisoning.

00:27:09.744 --> 00:27:12.813
But she won two Nobel prizes.

00:27:12.813 --> 00:27:17.485
First woman to win a Nobel
Prize, only woman to win two,

00:27:17.485 --> 00:27:20.021
and for only person
ever to win one

00:27:20.021 --> 00:27:23.558
in two different disciplines,
physics and chemistry.

00:27:23.558 --> 00:27:27.461
She was quite a
brilliant scientist.

00:27:27.461 --> 00:27:28.796
And they gave us this radiation.

00:27:28.796 --> 00:27:32.400
Now, OK, but see, OK, they
were really into radiation,

00:27:32.400 --> 00:27:35.770
but it was Rutherford who
said, well, wait a second.

00:27:35.770 --> 00:27:40.541
Maybe I can use this stuff to
keep going along this story.

00:27:40.541 --> 00:27:41.042
Right?

00:27:41.042 --> 00:27:42.910
Rutherford was like,
well, maybe there's

00:27:42.910 --> 00:27:45.379
something we could do
with this radiation.

00:27:45.379 --> 00:27:49.150
And so what happened?

00:27:49.150 --> 00:27:50.451
Oh, there it is!

00:27:50.451 --> 00:27:53.254
And so I'm going to put it
right above it so you can see.

00:27:53.254 --> 00:27:58.426
So he said, well, OK, if I take
something that glows, maybe

00:27:58.426 --> 00:28:01.829
some radium, and I carve
out a little thing here,

00:28:01.829 --> 00:28:06.067
and maybe I put some plates
to kind of protect scatter,

00:28:06.067 --> 00:28:10.404
I can make a beam
of this stuff, OK?

00:28:10.404 --> 00:28:15.109
So this would be like
maybe radium, let's say.

00:28:15.109 --> 00:28:17.645
And then what he did is he
took this photographic plate

00:28:17.645 --> 00:28:20.448
and he curved it, just so
he could really collect

00:28:20.448 --> 00:28:22.550
as much as you could, right?

00:28:22.550 --> 00:28:25.519
So you can really collect
at really high angles.

00:28:25.519 --> 00:28:26.988
And he did the same thing.

00:28:26.988 --> 00:28:31.492
He put charged plates
on either side,

00:28:31.492 --> 00:28:37.365
and he found that you had
three different types of beams.

00:28:37.365 --> 00:28:40.301
He had one that went like
this, one that went like this,

00:28:40.301 --> 00:28:41.469
and one that went like this.

00:28:41.469 --> 00:28:46.140
And we now know that this
is beta, this is gamma,

00:28:46.140 --> 00:28:47.942
and that's alpha.

00:28:47.942 --> 00:28:49.343
Let's put this right above.

00:28:49.343 --> 00:28:51.045
This is so cool to watch.

00:28:51.045 --> 00:28:53.514
So what Rutherford did
is kind of like this.

00:28:53.514 --> 00:28:56.284
He had stuff and he
made a beam of it,

00:28:56.284 --> 00:29:00.221
and then he put stuff around it.

00:29:00.221 --> 00:29:01.622
But if you got a
beam of something

00:29:01.622 --> 00:29:02.556
that might be
charged, you're not

00:29:02.556 --> 00:29:04.091
going to smash it or burn it.

00:29:04.091 --> 00:29:05.893
You're going to put
some plates around it,

00:29:05.893 --> 00:29:07.561
or a magnetic field
or electric field.

00:29:07.561 --> 00:29:09.196
And that's what he did.

00:29:09.196 --> 00:29:14.068
And notice, alpha bends down,
towards the positive charge,

00:29:14.068 --> 00:29:15.236
towards the negative charge.

00:29:15.236 --> 00:29:18.406
So alpha must be
positively charged.

00:29:18.406 --> 00:29:20.141
I said, OK, that sounds cool.

00:29:20.141 --> 00:29:22.943
Maybe I can use this.

00:29:22.943 --> 00:29:27.682
Maybe I can use this on
this, on this question

00:29:27.682 --> 00:29:30.951
of how these things are
spatially distributed.

00:29:30.951 --> 00:29:34.422
So then he said, OK,
let's just screen out

00:29:34.422 --> 00:29:35.656
those alpha particles.

00:29:35.656 --> 00:29:39.960
And let's just take these alpha
particles and let's shoot them.

00:29:39.960 --> 00:29:43.097
So now comes the
famous experiment,

00:29:43.097 --> 00:29:47.101
so you had stuff in here,
radium making a beam.

00:29:47.101 --> 00:29:51.672
And you collect it and you
make it just alpha particles.

00:29:51.672 --> 00:29:52.173
OK?

00:29:52.173 --> 00:29:57.611
So now he's just got all alpha
particles, and what he did

00:29:57.611 --> 00:30:00.381
is he also had a
photographic plate,

00:30:00.381 --> 00:30:02.116
or this could also be
a phosphorus screen,

00:30:02.116 --> 00:30:03.217
and you can watch where--

00:30:03.217 --> 00:30:07.488
You know, if it's a photographic
plate, you record events,

00:30:07.488 --> 00:30:09.757
and you can see it
like a picture, a film.

00:30:09.757 --> 00:30:13.127
And if it's a screen,
then you watch it.

00:30:13.127 --> 00:30:14.829
Either one was used.

00:30:14.829 --> 00:30:20.568
But what he did is he put a very
thin strip of gold in there.

00:30:20.568 --> 00:30:33.247
0.7 microns of gold, so that's
a 0.7 thickness is 0.7 microns.

00:30:33.247 --> 00:30:37.351
Really, really thin, like
a tissue paper of gold.

00:30:37.351 --> 00:30:39.019
And he shot these
alpha particles at it.

00:30:39.019 --> 00:30:40.821
Now, why is this important?

00:30:40.821 --> 00:30:45.059
Because-- gesundheit.

00:30:45.059 --> 00:30:50.865
Because if this model
of the atom was correct,

00:30:50.865 --> 00:30:54.168
then, you know, he'd isolated
these positive particles

00:30:54.168 --> 00:30:56.170
that he's shooting
at these atoms.

00:30:56.170 --> 00:30:59.106
If that model of
the atom is correct,

00:30:59.106 --> 00:31:03.644
then this is kind of like
distributed all over.

00:31:03.644 --> 00:31:04.178
Right?

00:31:04.178 --> 00:31:08.115
And so it's kind of neutral
almost anywhere you look.

00:31:08.115 --> 00:31:10.017
It's kind of neutral.

00:31:10.017 --> 00:31:14.121
And so you would expect that a
positive charge-- you'd maybe

00:31:14.121 --> 00:31:15.489
feel a little bit,
but it kind of

00:31:15.489 --> 00:31:17.725
feels mostly neutral because
it's evenly distributed.

00:31:17.725 --> 00:31:20.428
So it just kind of come out,
but that's not what he saw.

00:31:20.428 --> 00:31:22.129
That's not what he saw.

00:31:22.129 --> 00:31:25.866
What he saw is that
sometimes that would happen.

00:31:25.866 --> 00:31:27.635
You'd get a little
bit of deflection.

00:31:27.635 --> 00:31:30.371
And other times,
you'd get a signal

00:31:30.371 --> 00:31:38.546
out here, or there, or even
right back at the radium.

00:31:38.546 --> 00:31:41.148
How is that possible?

00:31:41.148 --> 00:31:42.049
This did not work.

00:31:42.049 --> 00:31:43.551
That model of the
atom did not work.

00:31:43.551 --> 00:31:47.688
And Rutherford himself said,
it was almost as incredible

00:31:47.688 --> 00:31:50.491
as if you fired a 15-inch shell
at a piece of tissue paper

00:31:50.491 --> 00:31:53.894
and it came back and hit
you, because they just

00:31:53.894 --> 00:31:57.832
didn't expect that a
positive particle could

00:31:57.832 --> 00:32:02.570
bounce backward off of an
even distribution of charge.

00:32:02.570 --> 00:32:06.640
And so this is a picture
from your textbook, Avril,

00:32:06.640 --> 00:32:09.877
that I know you are actively
reading on a daily basis.

00:32:09.877 --> 00:32:11.312
So there's the alpha particles.

00:32:11.312 --> 00:32:13.347
That's what I drew,
that they would expect--

00:32:13.347 --> 00:32:14.381
or that what I mentioned.

00:32:14.381 --> 00:32:16.650
They would expect it to
just kind of pass through.

00:32:16.650 --> 00:32:20.120
This is what was found,
where they kind of come back

00:32:20.120 --> 00:32:21.956
at very sharp angles.

00:32:21.956 --> 00:32:25.326
That's what he actually
observed, right?

00:32:25.326 --> 00:32:29.930
And it led to the
Rutherford atom.

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:32.533
And the Rutherford
atom was that, look,

00:32:32.533 --> 00:32:36.270
the only way that
this can happen,

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:39.240
that you get these events, where
a positive charge comes back

00:32:39.240 --> 00:32:42.877
like that, is if there's a
very, very strong concentration

00:32:42.877 --> 00:32:46.313
of the positive charge
in one small place.

00:32:46.313 --> 00:32:48.148
And so he said--

00:32:48.148 --> 00:32:49.984
let me see if I can
read my date correctly--

00:32:49.984 --> 00:32:52.987
1912.

00:32:52.987 --> 00:33:02.496
And so Rutherford, he said
that the positive charge

00:33:02.496 --> 00:33:06.033
has to be in the middle,
and then the electrons

00:33:06.033 --> 00:33:09.336
must be far away.

00:33:09.336 --> 00:33:14.909
And that is also sometimes, it
was called the planetary model.

00:33:14.909 --> 00:33:16.911
And in fact, there
was another scientist

00:33:16.911 --> 00:33:22.616
from Japan, Nangaioka, who, five
years earlier, had predicted--

00:33:22.616 --> 00:33:26.320
although he sort of predicted
this, because he loved Saturn.

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:27.821
[LAUGHTER]

00:33:27.821 --> 00:33:28.856
He really did.

00:33:28.856 --> 00:33:33.260
So he said, you know,
it seems that maybe we

00:33:33.260 --> 00:33:34.261
have a Saturn situation.

00:33:34.261 --> 00:33:35.829
So he called it the
Saturnian model.

00:33:35.829 --> 00:33:40.935
But I want to mention it because
it was part of the way there.

00:33:40.935 --> 00:33:42.036
Right?

00:33:42.036 --> 00:33:43.571
But in this planetary
model, which

00:33:43.571 --> 00:33:47.107
came from Rutherford's
experiments,

00:33:47.107 --> 00:33:49.677
you had a very different
picture now of the atom.

00:33:49.677 --> 00:33:51.111
You had a very
different picture.

00:33:51.111 --> 00:33:53.047
You had all the
positive charge really

00:33:53.047 --> 00:33:55.950
concentrated in the middle.

00:33:55.950 --> 00:34:01.722
Now, the thing is
also that the atom,

00:34:01.722 --> 00:34:03.257
what they were
starting to understand

00:34:03.257 --> 00:34:05.225
is that the atom isn't just--

00:34:05.225 --> 00:34:08.429
like, there isn't just
all this space here.

00:34:08.429 --> 00:34:09.463
Right?

00:34:09.463 --> 00:34:11.931
They knew the electrons weren't
distributed evenly in here.

00:34:11.931 --> 00:34:13.900
They knew they were
sort of far away.

00:34:13.900 --> 00:34:15.703
There is a lot of space.

00:34:15.703 --> 00:34:19.672
There's a lot of space,
that the radius of the atom

00:34:19.672 --> 00:34:26.112
is 100,000 times bigger than
the radius of the nucleus.

00:34:26.112 --> 00:34:27.614
Right?

00:34:27.614 --> 00:34:29.416
So just to put that
in perspective,

00:34:29.416 --> 00:34:30.650
so here's a picture.

00:34:30.650 --> 00:34:32.987
There would be the
nucleus, the head of a pin.

00:34:32.987 --> 00:34:36.489
And this would be
the atom, a stadium.

00:34:36.489 --> 00:34:37.458
OK?

00:34:37.458 --> 00:34:44.565
If you took all this stuff,
all the stuff in a human body--

00:34:44.565 --> 00:34:49.003
that means the
protons, all the stuff

00:34:49.003 --> 00:34:53.239
in the nuclei, the neutrons--
ooh, I'm getting there!

00:34:53.239 --> 00:34:57.544
And the electrons, and
you take 7 billion people,

00:34:57.544 --> 00:35:00.347
and you put that
all into one volume,

00:35:00.347 --> 00:35:03.517
it is the volume
of a sugar cube.

00:35:03.517 --> 00:35:07.688
So you know, so things are
pretty empty out there.

00:35:07.688 --> 00:35:09.123
Things are pretty
empty out there.

00:35:09.123 --> 00:35:10.557
How empty are they?

00:35:10.557 --> 00:35:13.293
But this is where you
find true meaning.

00:35:13.293 --> 00:35:14.895
This is deep!

00:35:14.895 --> 00:35:16.030
How empty is stuff?

00:35:16.030 --> 00:35:23.804
Look, if I weigh the universe,
you know, the universe itself,

00:35:23.804 --> 00:35:26.607
we're going from the atom and
the electron, all the way up

00:35:26.607 --> 00:35:27.508
to the universe.

00:35:27.508 --> 00:35:30.044
The universe weighs
something like, oh, 10

00:35:30.044 --> 00:35:33.747
to 60-ish kilograms.

00:35:33.747 --> 00:35:38.986
That's over roughly
$30 billion lightyears.

00:35:42.356 --> 00:35:46.060
OK, now, if we just do the math,
so you weighed the universe.

00:35:46.060 --> 00:35:47.828
You know how big it is, roughly.

00:35:47.828 --> 00:35:49.797
Plus of give or minus--

00:35:49.797 --> 00:35:50.964
plus or minus.

00:35:50.964 --> 00:36:00.774
Then that means that 10 to the
minus 20% of the whole universe

00:36:00.774 --> 00:36:01.275
is stuff.

00:36:03.911 --> 00:36:04.812
And the rest is empty.

00:36:09.650 --> 00:36:15.522
That's deep, because we find
meaning in all of it, don't we?

00:36:15.522 --> 00:36:16.256
That's deep.

00:36:16.256 --> 00:36:18.459
Think about that.

00:36:18.459 --> 00:36:21.395
That's not just the
universe, but the atom.

00:36:21.395 --> 00:36:22.096
It's the same.

00:36:22.096 --> 00:36:25.799
You know, so much of
everything is nothing.

00:36:29.203 --> 00:36:31.338
OK, planetaria, but
there was a problem

00:36:31.338 --> 00:36:33.107
with the planetary model.

00:36:33.107 --> 00:36:38.245
There was a big problem
with the planetary model,

00:36:38.245 --> 00:36:44.351
because classical ENM
told us that if, you know,

00:36:44.351 --> 00:36:48.822
if a charge is accelerating, if
a charge is accelerating, which

00:36:48.822 --> 00:36:51.158
it is, because it's ughhh!

00:36:51.158 --> 00:36:53.193
We think it's going around.

00:36:53.193 --> 00:36:55.462
We think they're
going around here.

00:36:55.462 --> 00:36:56.930
They are not!

00:36:56.930 --> 00:36:59.032
Friday, we'll know
more about that.

00:36:59.032 --> 00:37:00.667
But we thought they were.

00:37:00.667 --> 00:37:04.705
But if it's accelerating,
you know, to keep the circle,

00:37:04.705 --> 00:37:06.540
then it's got to be
radiating energy.

00:37:06.540 --> 00:37:08.342
That's what classical
ENM tells us.

00:37:08.342 --> 00:37:10.277
An accelerating
charge loses energy.

00:37:10.277 --> 00:37:16.016
So if you go with that,
then the electron--

00:37:16.016 --> 00:37:19.620
let's see, the stability
analysis that we get out

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:22.122
of something like that--

00:37:22.122 --> 00:37:31.431
hang on-- is that the
atom would be stable,

00:37:31.431 --> 00:37:42.009
four-ish 10 to the minus 11
seconds, five times, roughly.

00:37:42.009 --> 00:37:45.245
That's not giving
us a lot of time.

00:37:45.245 --> 00:37:45.746
All right?

00:37:45.746 --> 00:37:47.447
And so we were getting so far.

00:37:47.447 --> 00:37:52.886
We had this great model, but it
didn't go with classical ENM.

00:37:52.886 --> 00:38:01.595
So atom's stable for that
with classical ENM, oh!

00:38:01.595 --> 00:38:02.963
Because you know what's coming.

00:38:06.767 --> 00:38:08.101
We're going quantum.

00:38:08.101 --> 00:38:11.605
Not now, I'm just
preparing you for later--

00:38:11.605 --> 00:38:13.240
Did I hear an oh?

00:38:13.240 --> 00:38:13.941
Thank you.

00:38:13.941 --> 00:38:15.242
Friday.

00:38:15.242 --> 00:38:17.711
OK, so Bohr comes along,
and on Wednesday, we're

00:38:17.711 --> 00:38:20.781
really going to go into Bohr.

00:38:20.781 --> 00:38:22.449
And we're going to
talk about how Bohr's

00:38:22.449 --> 00:38:26.386
model, how Bohr's
model of the atom

00:38:26.386 --> 00:38:30.958
allowed us to understand how
light and matter interacts.

00:38:30.958 --> 00:38:34.494
That's a big deal, right?

00:38:34.494 --> 00:38:35.996
But we'll go into
that on Wednesday.

00:38:35.996 --> 00:38:37.531
For now, I just want
to tell you what

00:38:37.531 --> 00:38:41.635
he did, which is he thought
about this problem a lot.

00:38:41.635 --> 00:38:44.371
He's like, this can't be.

00:38:44.371 --> 00:38:46.640
We need some way out of this.

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:51.879
And he wrote this paper,
where he said the following.

00:38:51.879 --> 00:38:53.914
He said, let's go over here.

00:38:53.914 --> 00:38:56.783
He said, OK, "In
order to explain

00:38:56.783 --> 00:39:00.888
the results of experiments on
scattering of alpha rays"--

00:39:00.888 --> 00:39:04.157
we just did that,
scattering of alpha rays--

00:39:04.157 --> 00:39:06.994
"by matter, Professor
Rutherford has given a theory

00:39:06.994 --> 00:39:08.128
of the structure of atoms."

00:39:08.128 --> 00:39:10.964
And then Bohr goes on.

00:39:10.964 --> 00:39:15.269
And he says, "Great
interest is to be

00:39:15.269 --> 00:39:17.704
attributed to this atom model."

00:39:17.704 --> 00:39:21.208
Now, that is a serious diss.

00:39:21.208 --> 00:39:22.409
Great interest?

00:39:22.409 --> 00:39:26.213
If somebody calls your
model of great interest,

00:39:26.213 --> 00:39:29.316
you know you are in trouble.

00:39:29.316 --> 00:39:31.618
He might as well
have said back then,

00:39:31.618 --> 00:39:34.521
you know, this model
is of great interest

00:39:34.521 --> 00:39:35.822
because it's totally wrong!

00:39:35.822 --> 00:39:37.557
[LAUGHTER]

00:39:37.557 --> 00:39:39.726
And if you want to know
what's up, come over to me

00:39:39.726 --> 00:39:41.695
and I'll tell you.

00:39:41.695 --> 00:39:45.065
And that's what Bohr
helped with in his work.

00:39:45.065 --> 00:39:48.869
And what he did is he
wrote down some postulates.

00:39:48.869 --> 00:39:52.539
He said, look, the
Rutherford atom is correct,

00:39:52.539 --> 00:39:56.610
but the problem here is
classical ENM theory.

00:39:56.610 --> 00:39:57.344
Right?

00:39:57.344 --> 00:40:01.682
You cannot apply classical
ENM theory to the orbiting

00:40:01.682 --> 00:40:02.683
electron.

00:40:02.683 --> 00:40:04.851
You can't, because
that's what happened.

00:40:04.851 --> 00:40:07.454
So something's wrong
with literally nothing

00:40:07.454 --> 00:40:10.290
less than classical physics.

00:40:10.290 --> 00:40:11.625
Right?

00:40:11.625 --> 00:40:14.394
He said, Newtonian mechanics
still works, right?

00:40:14.394 --> 00:40:17.331
Newtonian mechanics
works, but not ENM.

00:40:17.331 --> 00:40:19.032
So he said, we can
go classical on how

00:40:19.032 --> 00:40:20.267
things are moving around.

00:40:20.267 --> 00:40:23.804
F equals MA stuff, but not ENM.

00:40:23.804 --> 00:40:27.908
He also postulated
that the energy

00:40:27.908 --> 00:40:31.745
of these electrons, the energy
that these electrons sit at

00:40:31.745 --> 00:40:33.013
or have--

00:40:33.013 --> 00:40:36.149
we'll get into this a lot--

00:40:36.149 --> 00:40:39.720
he hypothesized that
that's quantized.

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:41.421
Quantized.

00:40:41.421 --> 00:40:43.323
Quantized.

00:40:43.323 --> 00:40:44.691
We're saying that word.

00:40:44.691 --> 00:40:45.826
It's a beautiful moment.

00:40:45.826 --> 00:40:46.626
Quantized.

00:40:46.626 --> 00:40:49.396
Quantized means
that it could only

00:40:49.396 --> 00:40:55.035
have certain values,
certain values, which

00:40:55.035 --> 00:40:57.604
means that if this electron
were to be in one value

00:40:57.604 --> 00:41:02.309
and then change into another,
that the transitions are

00:41:02.309 --> 00:41:03.110
quantized.

00:41:03.110 --> 00:41:08.382
The transitions of an
electron from one energy

00:41:08.382 --> 00:41:11.918
level of the atom to another
can only have certain values.

00:41:11.918 --> 00:41:14.888
And that is what we get
from the Bohr model.

00:41:14.888 --> 00:41:16.790
And boy, did it explain things!

00:41:16.790 --> 00:41:20.627
That's going to be the subject
of Wednesday, as I mentioned.

00:41:20.627 --> 00:41:21.128
Right?

00:41:21.128 --> 00:41:28.702
But this gets us
to the Bohr model.

00:41:28.702 --> 00:41:31.138
And here's Bohr.

00:41:31.138 --> 00:41:33.807
We're now-- oh, I
got to draw this.

00:41:33.807 --> 00:41:36.643
We got all the positive
charge in the middle,

00:41:36.643 --> 00:41:44.051
and then these electrons
can only have certain radii,

00:41:44.051 --> 00:41:46.053
certain energies.

00:41:46.053 --> 00:41:49.122
They cannot continuously
roam around.

00:41:49.122 --> 00:41:49.923
They can't.

00:41:49.923 --> 00:41:50.724
They're quantized.

00:41:50.724 --> 00:41:52.926
That's the Bohr model.

00:41:52.926 --> 00:42:01.802
And this is closed in 1912.

00:42:01.802 --> 00:42:07.808
This is closing in on the
last and final model, which

00:42:07.808 --> 00:42:10.377
we'll get to this week, which
is where quantum mechanics comes

00:42:10.377 --> 00:42:11.645
in.

00:42:11.645 --> 00:42:15.048
OK, now, we got one more
thing to talk about.

00:42:15.048 --> 00:42:16.850
So this is where we're going.

00:42:16.850 --> 00:42:21.521
We're building up the full
understanding of the atom.

00:42:21.521 --> 00:42:24.825
But there's one more thing
that I've got to come back to,

00:42:24.825 --> 00:42:27.194
and that is that,
look, we just said

00:42:27.194 --> 00:42:28.929
there are these positive
charges in there

00:42:28.929 --> 00:42:30.730
and these negative
charges out there.

00:42:30.730 --> 00:42:34.101
But it turns out that atoms
can have different masses

00:42:34.101 --> 00:42:35.769
and stay neutral.

00:42:35.769 --> 00:42:36.269
All right?

00:42:36.269 --> 00:42:39.239
So we're now in a position
to talk about this thing

00:42:39.239 --> 00:42:41.708
that we've been calling
the nucleus and the fact

00:42:41.708 --> 00:42:44.111
that it's not just protons.

00:42:44.111 --> 00:42:45.979
We're in a position
to talk about that.

00:42:45.979 --> 00:42:48.682
And so, you know,
Rutherford gave us

00:42:48.682 --> 00:42:53.386
this idea of the proton,
the particle, the proton.

00:42:53.386 --> 00:42:57.157
And it wasn't until
12 years later

00:42:57.157 --> 00:43:01.161
that Chadwick found the neutron.

00:43:01.161 --> 00:43:08.135
What the neutron does is
it adds mass to the atom.

00:43:08.135 --> 00:43:10.604
See, the mass didn't work.

00:43:10.604 --> 00:43:14.441
So again, you know, if you know
what the mass of the proton is,

00:43:14.441 --> 00:43:17.410
then you can add it up, and you
get the mass of the electron,

00:43:17.410 --> 00:43:20.080
although it's like
1,000th as much.

00:43:20.080 --> 00:43:21.214
But it doesn't work.

00:43:21.214 --> 00:43:23.350
There's something else in
there that's adding mass.

00:43:23.350 --> 00:43:25.852
But it's not adding charge,
because these things

00:43:25.852 --> 00:43:28.355
are electrically neutral.

00:43:28.355 --> 00:43:30.824
So those other particles
are called neutrons,

00:43:30.824 --> 00:43:37.564
and what it does is it gives us
ranges of masses for the atoms.

00:43:37.564 --> 00:43:38.064
Right?

00:43:38.064 --> 00:43:39.799
It gives us ranges.

00:43:39.799 --> 00:43:43.303
So because, you
know, hydrogen now.

00:43:43.303 --> 00:43:46.540
If you think about it,
99.99% of all hydrogen

00:43:46.540 --> 00:43:50.410
is just that thing on the
left, a proton and an electron.

00:43:50.410 --> 00:43:52.345
But sometimes, it has a neutron.

00:43:52.345 --> 00:43:53.079
So it weighs more.

00:43:53.079 --> 00:43:54.481
It's still got neutral charge.

00:43:54.481 --> 00:43:56.683
It's called deuterium.

00:43:56.683 --> 00:43:58.652
And then you've got
tritium, which is unstable.

00:43:58.652 --> 00:44:01.021
You can make it.

00:44:01.021 --> 00:44:03.490
And these are called isotopes,
as many of you, I'm sure,

00:44:03.490 --> 00:44:04.224
already know.

00:44:04.224 --> 00:44:12.465
These are called isotopes, and
so the isotopes of an atom,

00:44:12.465 --> 00:44:33.453
the isotopes of an atom
with the same atomic number,

00:44:33.453 --> 00:44:43.863
different number of neutrons,
different number of neutrons.

00:44:43.863 --> 00:44:46.199
So atoms have these
three things in them.

00:44:46.199 --> 00:44:48.535
They've got the neutrons, the
protons, and the electrons.

00:44:48.535 --> 00:44:51.304
We've been talking today about
the protons and the electrons,

00:44:51.304 --> 00:44:53.306
but they got to have these
other things in them,

00:44:53.306 --> 00:44:54.741
or the mass doesn't work.

00:44:54.741 --> 00:44:58.979
And so if you look at
something like carbon,

00:44:58.979 --> 00:44:59.980
you see the same thing.

00:44:59.980 --> 00:45:01.948
And now I want to get
into how these things are

00:45:01.948 --> 00:45:03.950
written, right?

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:07.354
Because here's carbon, and
there are those electrons.

00:45:07.354 --> 00:45:08.622
They're now put out there.

00:45:08.622 --> 00:45:10.657
OK, we didn't draw
circles, so maybe this

00:45:10.657 --> 00:45:15.662
is around Rutherford's time
when Avril made this picture.

00:45:15.662 --> 00:45:19.032
And there is the pluses, and
there's other things in there.

00:45:19.032 --> 00:45:19.733
Right?

00:45:19.733 --> 00:45:20.867
And those are the neutrons.

00:45:20.867 --> 00:45:24.938
And so what we do is we have
a nomenclature for this,

00:45:24.938 --> 00:45:26.373
which is the standard.

00:45:26.373 --> 00:45:31.111
And that is that if we
write an element like this,

00:45:31.111 --> 00:45:32.212
this would be the element.

00:45:34.748 --> 00:45:39.019
And we put something here
and something here, this

00:45:39.019 --> 00:45:43.189
is the mass number.

00:45:43.189 --> 00:45:44.424
OK?

00:45:44.424 --> 00:45:48.161
So that's how many
protons plus neutrons.

00:45:51.264 --> 00:45:53.033
Neu-- ah!

00:45:53.033 --> 00:45:54.701
Neutrons.

00:45:54.701 --> 00:45:56.736
And this is called
the atomic number.

00:45:59.439 --> 00:46:01.641
Now, the atomic number,
I've been referencing

00:46:01.641 --> 00:46:03.009
this thing, this atomic number.

00:46:03.009 --> 00:46:05.045
I've said, Mendeleev
and many others,

00:46:05.045 --> 00:46:08.448
they put things in
order of atomic number,

00:46:08.448 --> 00:46:10.350
maybe mass sometimes.

00:46:10.350 --> 00:46:12.986
Mendeleev did the thing
where he did those things

00:46:12.986 --> 00:46:15.689
and properties, right?

00:46:15.689 --> 00:46:20.593
And the atomic number was
just a number, until later.

00:46:20.593 --> 00:46:23.530
And once we discover
another type of ray,

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:28.234
x-rays, then this has real
physical meaning, right?

00:46:28.234 --> 00:46:29.903
But right now, it's
still just a number.

00:46:29.903 --> 00:46:33.406
It's a number of the element
in the periodic table.

00:46:33.406 --> 00:46:33.907
Right?

00:46:33.907 --> 00:46:38.211
But that mass number tells
me how many neutrons I have

00:46:38.211 --> 00:46:39.546
and what the isotope is.

00:46:39.546 --> 00:46:41.514
So now I go back.

00:46:41.514 --> 00:46:45.285
I can go back, and I
can look at carbon.

00:46:45.285 --> 00:46:47.520
Oh, by the way, carbon--

00:46:47.520 --> 00:46:50.357
I wanted to write this
down before I say that.

00:46:50.357 --> 00:46:58.631
Carbon, when you look it up in
the table, you have 12.011 AMU,

00:46:58.631 --> 00:46:59.366
or grams per mole.

00:46:59.366 --> 00:47:01.101
That's what's in the table.

00:47:01.101 --> 00:47:04.671
But now you know why,
because it's just an average.

00:47:04.671 --> 00:47:07.574
And in fact, the IUPAC,
which is a rocking body

00:47:07.574 --> 00:47:11.077
of the International Union of
Pure and Applied Chemistry,

00:47:11.077 --> 00:47:13.980
I'm sure you've seen
them on the feeds.

00:47:13.980 --> 00:47:18.184
They hold meetings and in
Vienna on a nonstop night

00:47:18.184 --> 00:47:21.554
of intense meeting and partying.

00:47:21.554 --> 00:47:25.024
And they said,
hey, maybe in 2011,

00:47:25.024 --> 00:47:27.394
maybe we should actually do
this with the periodic table,

00:47:27.394 --> 00:47:29.229
just to show that
actually there really is

00:47:29.229 --> 00:47:31.398
a range of-- if I take
carbon out of the ground here

00:47:31.398 --> 00:47:32.799
or carbon out of
the ground there,

00:47:32.799 --> 00:47:35.101
maybe they have
different AMUs, right?

00:47:35.101 --> 00:47:37.670
These are averages,
and there are ranges.

00:47:37.670 --> 00:47:40.306
So they're actually talking
about different ways

00:47:40.306 --> 00:47:45.011
of even putting this into
periodic tables, right?

00:47:45.011 --> 00:47:45.812
But this is it.

00:47:45.812 --> 00:47:47.380
And this gets us back.

00:47:47.380 --> 00:47:51.951
Oh, there's boron and
nitrogen ranges, right?

00:47:51.951 --> 00:47:56.956
But what we do now, at least, is
we just look at the one number,

00:47:56.956 --> 00:47:59.192
because that's sort
of averaged over,

00:47:59.192 --> 00:48:01.494
you know, basically anywhere
you can dig this stuff up.

00:48:01.494 --> 00:48:03.730
And the stable isotopes.

00:48:03.730 --> 00:48:04.697
Stable isotopes.

00:48:04.697 --> 00:48:07.834
And it gets me to
my last slide, which

00:48:07.834 --> 00:48:10.804
just comes back to the
beginning, as I promised.

00:48:10.804 --> 00:48:11.538
Right?

00:48:11.538 --> 00:48:13.940
Because now I can say not only
how many gallium atoms are

00:48:13.940 --> 00:48:15.508
in this strip, but
I can tell you,

00:48:15.508 --> 00:48:17.110
gallium has two stable isotopes.

00:48:17.110 --> 00:48:19.179
By the way, it's got a
bunch of unstable ones,

00:48:19.179 --> 00:48:23.616
but it's got two stable ones,
gallium 69 and gallium 71.

00:48:23.616 --> 00:48:26.886
And I can now ask you,
how many gallium 69 atoms

00:48:26.886 --> 00:48:27.987
are in this strip?

00:48:27.987 --> 00:48:28.788
How?

00:48:28.788 --> 00:48:35.462
Because I can again look
up in the periodic table

00:48:35.462 --> 00:48:40.767
and find that in
a gallium strip,

00:48:40.767 --> 00:48:42.669
I know how many atoms I have.

00:48:42.669 --> 00:48:49.309
But if I say, well, 69
times x plus 71 times 1

00:48:49.309 --> 00:48:57.116
minus x, that's going
to equal 69.723 AMU.

00:48:57.116 --> 00:48:58.618
That's what's in
the periodic table.

00:48:58.618 --> 00:49:00.220
But I now know
that that's simply

00:49:00.220 --> 00:49:01.988
an average over all the--

00:49:01.988 --> 00:49:02.489
right?

00:49:02.489 --> 00:49:11.898
So this would be the
fraction of 69 GA, right?

00:49:11.898 --> 00:49:14.567
Because that's how simple these
isotope problems are, right?

00:49:14.567 --> 00:49:17.871
It's simply averaging over the
potentially stable isotopes.

00:49:17.871 --> 00:49:21.608
And I leave you with
one more great mystery.

00:49:21.608 --> 00:49:28.214
As you are pondering
this, think about this.

00:49:28.214 --> 00:49:31.551
There are two elements, ah!

00:49:31.551 --> 00:49:37.156
There are two elements for which
there are no stable isotopes.

00:49:37.156 --> 00:49:39.425
Technetium and promethium.

00:49:39.425 --> 00:49:41.127
Nothing!

00:49:41.127 --> 00:49:42.729
Technetium, we use all the time.

00:49:42.729 --> 00:49:45.431
80% of all MRIs use technetium,
but it's not stable.

00:49:45.431 --> 00:49:47.400
We got to make it, right?

00:49:47.400 --> 00:49:48.601
That's pretty deep too.

00:49:48.601 --> 00:49:49.802
How did that happen?

00:49:49.802 --> 00:49:52.405
Why did that happen?

00:49:52.405 --> 00:49:55.074
For the answer, you'll
have to stay tuned.

00:49:55.074 --> 00:49:59.012
Have a good night, and
see you guys on Wednesday.

